From spencer.graves at pdf.com  Tue Jun  1 11:58:02 2004
From: spencer.graves at pdf.com (Spencer Graves)
Date: Tue, 01 Jun 2004 04:58:02 -0700
Subject: Re: Non-Linear Optimizers --- A comparison with a Stochastic
In-Reply-To: <410-220046213218187@earthlink.net>
References: <410-220046213218187@earthlink.net>
Message-ID: <40BC6F4A.5000902@pdf.com>

     Have you considered eliminating the constraints?  I routinely 3 
techniques for this:  First, with equality constraints, it is sometimes 
feasible to reduce the number of parameters to be estimated.  Second, 
inequality constraints can sometimes be handled by sending the 
constraint to +/-Inf.  Third, both equalities and inequalities can 
sometimes be handled by converting the nasty constraints into a penalty 
added to the objective function proportion to the square of the 
violation.  I use the squares, because then the objective function is 
differentiable.  I may solve the same system with 2 or 3 different 
levels of the penalty:  If the penalty is too large, the system is stiff 
and difficult to optimize, so it may make sense to start with a modest 
penalty.  After I have something close to a sensible answer, I increase 
the penalty to polish the result. 

     If I have trouble with an objective function that returns NAs in 
certain cases, I identify those cases and use appropriate asymptotic 
theory (at least l'Hopital's rule and preferably something like a Taylor 
expansion) to replace 0/0 with a sensible number. 

     If you do this, then you can use all the alternative methods in 
optim.  I found that none of the 4 consistently outperform the others, 
though simulated annealing was only rarely better than the others.  The 
documentation for Nelder-Mead admits that it is adaptive and that it is 
often wise to restart using the final results.  I often start with one 
method, and switch to another.  If you have 400 of these to do, it might 
make sense to do a designed experiment, starting with one method, then 
switching to a second, and evaluating the alternative methods on the 
level of improvement after the switch.  Then you could do a final pass 
on all 400 using whichever method seems to show the greatest post-switch 
improvement overall. 

     hope this helps.  spencer graves

Paul Lasky wrote:

> I've recentally had an opportunity to compare  and stress  the 2
> available non-linear optimizers available to S+ and R users: MASS's
> optim, and Splus's nlminb.  In actual practice, optim far out
> performed nlminb.
>
>
>  I've been trying to solve a  difficult, 22 variable Stochastic
> Programming problem, that involves an objective function that
> includes a nasty barrier function possessing an abrupt, if not
> discontinuous, first derivative.  The problem involves minimizing the
> objective function subject to a number of equality constraints.
>
>
>  Because optim  and nlminb  only allow box constraints, I eliminated
> the equality constraints with use of the "Breaking Stick"
> parameterization so that the transformed variables only are
> constrained by box constraints.
>
>
> In use, nlminb  broke down after about 5 minutes of running (on a 3
> ghz Win  Xp machine) and gave up with the odd comment that "a
> singularity had been reached." Probably difficulty computing a
> numerical derivative or Hessian of the barrier function.
>
>
>  However optim  readily solved the same problem in about  3 minutes
> utilizing the "L-BFGS-B" method. The comparison of the 2 optimizers
> was repeated with different input parameters with the same result.  I
> now have to only solve about 400 more problems to complete the
> Stochastic sampling...
>
>
> I found the result surprising since I believed that both routines
> utilized the same standard newton method with a barrier function to
> account for the box constraints.
>
>
> It would be much better if an interior point routine that could handle
> inequality constraints was available to Splus and R users, but outside
> of NUOPT, I can't find one. (Breaking Stick parameteriztions can be
> unstable because they depend on the order of the variables.)  NUOPT
> is very touchy, failing to compile many objective functions and it
> appears to be extremely limited because it does not utilize Splus to
> program the objective and inequalities. Further in its language the
> function set is very limited.  Ability to program
> non-summable objective functions and complicated inequalities is very
> limited in NUOPT.
>
>
> To effectively solve Stochastic Programming problems the optimizer
> should be placed  inside a loop on the stochastic inequality
> parameters;  use of optim in a loop is  only barely feasible because
> of its lack of speed.
>
> Any suggestions to help out with this calculational problem would be
> welcome.
>
