From Kim.Elmore at noaa.gov  Tue Jun  8 02:23:23 2004
From: Kim.Elmore at noaa.gov (Kim Elmore)
Date: Mon, 07 Jun 2004 21:23:23 -0500
Subject: Variable Expressions
Message-ID: <5.2.1.1.2.20040607211627.02717d68@127.0.0.1>

Dear fellow users,

I've come across an odd-problem that I initially thought would be easy to 
solve.

I'm reading in a large number of ASCII files into an equally large number 
of S-Plus data objects.  I have two character vectors whose elements of 
equal length: one contains the input file names and the other contains the 
names I want to use for the S-Plus objects.

It's easy to build and expression that pastes together the input file name 
on the right side of an expression, but I'm finding it a problem to 
simultaneously build the target object name on the left side of the expression.

Unfortunately, someone has walked off with my copy of "Programming in S and 
S-Plus," and I'm more benighted than usual without it. Hence my query: how 
do I build an expression that uses pasted-in names onboth sides?

Kim Elmore

From spencer.graves at pdf.com  Tue Jun  8 03:05:10 2004
From: spencer.graves at pdf.com (Spencer Graves)
Date: Mon, 07 Jun 2004 20:05:10 -0700
Subject: Re: Variable Expressions
In-Reply-To: <5.2.1.1.2.20040607211627.02717d68@127.0.0.1>
References: <5.2.1.1.2.20040607211627.02717d68@127.0.0.1>
Message-ID: <40C52CE6.5050902@pdf.com>

     The real wizards might do something with "do.call" and apply or 
lapply, but consider the following: 

> for(i in 1:2) {
   s.expression <- paste(letters[i], "<-", i)
   eval(parse(text = s.expression))
}
> a
[1] 1
> b
[1] 2
> for(i in 1:2) {
   s.expression <- paste(letters[i], "<-", i)
   eval(parse(text = s.expression))
}
> a
[1] 1
> b
[1] 2

     With this, I think you can probably do more or less whatever you 
want. 

     hope this helps.  spencer graves

