References: <1C0CCEE45B00E74D8FBBB1AE6A472E85040FFFBA@COBMAIL.ad.cob.ohio-state.edu> <1C0CCEE45B00E74D8FBBB1AE6A472E85040FFFBA@COBMAIL.ad.cob.oh io-state.edu>
Message-ID: <6.1.0.6.2.20040621120324.04072c00@mailhost.blackmesacapital.com>

Depends on how you define "problem"... (and I believe that on widely 
accepted technical definitions, this is not a problem with "the newest 
Intel processor architecture")

My experience with the issue discussed here is that on some Intel 
processors, some of the fast basic linear algebra routines in Intel's MKL 
library can return a non-symmetric matrix when they should be returning a 
perfectly symmetric matrix.  The asymmetry is at the level of rounding 
error and is thus insignificant to all intents and purposes.  However, the 
Fortran routine that chol() calls insists upon exact symmetry.  I'm not 
exactly sure how these asymmetries arise, but one possibility is that they 
result from intermediate results being kept in 80bit floating point 
registers rather than being rounded to regular double precision.  I'm by no 
means an expert on the IEEE floating point standard (754), but I'm fairly 
sure that it permits such things.

I'd be more inclined to blame the Fortran routine that insists on exact 
symmetry, and/or the use of it, or the way it is called.  The 
(undocumented) S-plus function choleski() works fine with these 
non-symmetric matrices -- Insightful support is probably happy to tell you 
how to use it instead of chol().  Alternatively, turning off use of the MKL 
library with the S-plus function call intelmkl.use(F) might help (see 
"Improving speed" in Chapter 21 of the Programmer's guide -- page 921 in my 
copy).  But this will make some computations run much slower.

-- Tony Plate

At Monday 10:59 AM 6/21/2004, Stahel, Christof wrote:
> There seems to be a problem with the newest Intel processor
> architecture. The chol() is based on a Fortran routine that has a lower
> tolerance than other Choleski decomposition functions. Simply use those
> instead.
