From andy_liaw at merck.com  Wed Jun 23 15:27:02 2004
From: andy_liaw at merck.com (Liaw, Andy)
Date: Wed, 23 Jun 2004 11:27:02 -0400
Subject: Re: FW: help with S-Plus
In-Reply-To: <005101c45929$07bdb1a0$9f886444@D7CYFG11>
Message-ID: <3A822319EB35174CA3714066D590DCD504AF7F55@usrymx25.merck.com>

Look at the `Value' section of the help page for summary.aov.  It returns an
object of class `anova', which is really a data frame.  So "Pr(F)" is just a
column of that data frame.  Since this is a oneway ANOVA, there's only two
rows in the ANOVA table (one for the factor and another for the residual),
the object has two rows.  The row corresponding to residual does not have
p-value, so it's filled in with NA.  For more complicated models the object
can have more rows, obviously.

The reason that result2$Pr(F) generates an error is because S-PLUS
interprets it as calling the function `result2$Pr' with the argument `F',
which is not what was intended.  You need to quote names with special
characters such as `('; e.g., result2$"Pr(F)" would have worked.  The reason
that result2$Pr works is because the function `$' which extracts component
from a list (which data frame is) does partial matching.  If you supplied
enough characters to identify the component uniquely, it will work.  Try:

df <- data.frame(very.long.name=1:5)
df$very

HTH,
Andy

